"""Applies the editorial revision to a copy of the verified Springer main.tex.
Every replacement is asserted; numbers are copied from the verified text, not retyped from memory."""
t = open("../springer_cicps_submission/main.tex").read()
def seg(a, b):
    i = t.index(a); j = t.index(b, i) + len(b); return t[i:j]
R = []
R.append((seg("Kernel principal component analysis is often paired", "limited to one sample of one dataset."),
r"""Kernel principal component analysis (KPCA) is often paired with classical classifiers for network intrusion detection, but gains are usually reported on a single split and without a no-reduction baseline. We compare the 78 original CIC-IDS2017 flow features with PCA and KPCA (linear, RBF and cosine kernels; 5 to 30 components) across five classifiers: logistic regression, $k$-nearest neighbors, SVM, decision tree and random forest. The data are a class-stratified sample of 6,000 records (at least 100 per class) drawn after exact duplicate removal; the sample does not preserve natural traffic prevalence. Imputation, scaling and reduction are fitted on training folds only. Evaluation combines repeated stratified cross-validation, nested cross-validation, a small nested hyper-parameter search, two row-order checks that serve as an unverified proxy for chronology (the files contain no timestamps), and a runtime and memory benchmark. A random forest on the original features scored highest (macro-F1 $0.901\pm0.020$; accuracy $0.972\pm0.004$; nested estimate $0.900\pm0.022$), yet two web-attack classes had F1 of only 0.53 and 0.59. Reduction did not help the decision tree or random forest and at best matched the original features for logistic regression and $k$-NN. Only the SVM gained from reduction (cosine KPCA), and that gain fell from 0.076 to 0.021 after tuning. In our benchmark, KPCA fitting took more than 200 times longer than PCA and used about 37 times more traced memory. The results come from one sample of one dataset, and every significance test carries a fold-dependence caveat."""))
R.append(("Most report accuracy on one split; none that we read pairs a no-reduction baseline, per-class analysis and fold-wise fitting across PCA and several KPCA kernels.",
r"Among the studies we read, none pairs a no-reduction baseline, per-class analysis and fold-wise fitting across PCA and several KPCA kernels. Our reading was not exhaustive, so this is not a claim about a gap in the literature."))
R.append(("We use the \\emph{original} labelled flows, so absolute scores may partly reflect such artefacts.",
r"These papers bear on how far absolute scores can be trusted, not on dimensionality reduction itself. We use the \emph{original} labelled flows, so our absolute scores may partly reflect such artefacts."))
R.append((seg("Predicting the majority class gives accuracy 0.672", "These are the weak points that accuracy hides (Table~\\ref{tab:perclass})."),
r"""A majority-class predictor already scores accuracy 0.672, with macro-F1 0.054 and MCC 0.000. The random forest on the original features reaches accuracy $0.972\pm0.004$ but macro-F1 $0.901\pm0.020$ (weighted-F1 0.971, balanced accuracy 0.883, MCC $0.948\pm0.008$). The 0.071 gap between accuracy and macro-F1 comes from a few classes (Table~\ref{tab:perclass}). Ten of the 15 classes, among them benign, DDoS, the four DoS families, both Patator classes, PortScan and Heartbleed, have F1 $\ge$ 0.96. Web brute force (F1 0.53) and web XSS (0.59) are the weakest, and they are mostly confused with each other: 48 of 103 brute-force records are predicted as XSS, and 35 of 101 XSS records as brute force. Bot (0.87; recall 0.82), infiltration (0.875; recall 0.78 on 36 records) and SQL injection (0.84; 21 records) also fall short. Engelen et al.\ report that most web brute-force and XSS flows carry no payload in the forward direction \cite{engelen2021}; we did not test whether this explains the confusion here."""))
R.append((seg("Table~\\ref{tab:n10} and Fig.~\\ref{fig:all} compare spaces at $n=10$.", "and 0.746 at 30."),
r"""Table~\ref{tab:n10} and Fig.~\ref{fig:all} compare the feature spaces at $n=10$. Reduction usually hurt. The original space beat PCA-10 for logistic regression, SVM, decision tree and random forest, and beat RBF-10 and cosine-10 for all five classifiers; for $k$-NN it was indistinguishable from PCA-10 ($\Delta$ macro-F1 $-0.001$). Of the 30 pairwise comparisons, 23 had Holm-adjusted $p<0.05$. Because folds share training data, we read these as screening evidence, not proof. Among the reduced spaces, RBF-KPCA was worse than PCA for LR, $k$-NN, DT and RF but better for the SVM; cosine KPCA was better than PCA for the SVM ($+0.120$) and worse for LR, $k$-NN and DT; and linear KPCA never differed from PCA. The Friedman tests were all significant (Holm-adjusted $p\le 2.7\times10^{-9}$) and carry the same caveat.

A stricter question is whether each classifier's \emph{best} reduced space matches the original (table in the repository). These best spaces were picked on the same folds, which favours reduction. For DT ($+0.070$) and RF ($+0.056$) the original features were still better, in all 15 folds ($p_\mathrm{Holm}=0.0003$ for both). For logistic regression the best reduced space (PCA, 30 components) came within 0.004 ($p_\mathrm{Holm}=0.021$, significant but negligible), and for $k$-NN there was no difference. The SVM is the one case where reduction helped: cosine KPCA with 30 components raised macro-F1 from 0.675 to 0.746 ($p_\mathrm{Holm}=0.0003$, better in all 15 folds). That gain needs many components; at 10 components cosine KPCA scores 0.605, below the original 0.675."""))
R.append((seg("Table~\\ref{tab:tuning} reports the nested search", "not as a robust benefit of KPCA."),
r"""Table~\ref{tab:tuning} reports the nested search (five outer folds). Tuning barely changed the random forest (original 0.901 default, 0.899 tuned; best reduced 0.842 versus 0.839). For $k$-NN it added $+0.007$ on original features, and the gap to the best reduced space stayed within 0.003: 0.000 under defaults (cosine KPCA-30), $-0.002$ after tuning (PCA-30), and $+0.003$ for PCA-30 under defaults. All of these are far below the fold variation. The SVM changed most. Tuned macro-F1 rose from 0.668 to 0.791 on original features and from 0.745 to 0.812 with cosine KPCA-30, so the advantage of reduction shrank from 0.076 to 0.021. Five outer folds cannot establish a difference of 0.021, and the selected values sit on grid edges ($C=10$ for the SVM and $k=3$ for $k$-NN in all 20 selections). We therefore read the SVM advantage of KPCA as a property of the default RBF-SVM that largely disappears with tuning, not as a robust benefit of KPCA."""))
R.append((seg("Linear KPCA equals PCA on centred data", "the best model stayed the unweighted RF."),
r"""Linear KPCA reproduced PCA, as expected on centred data: results were identical for $k$-NN and SVM and within 0.001 (LR) or 0.007 (trees; small eigen-solver differences, not investigated). For LR and SVM the number of components mattered more than the kernel (LR macro-F1 rose from 0.37 at 5 PCA components to 0.73 at 30, against 0.736 on original features). For $k$-NN, DT and RF the kernel mattered somewhat more, but the effects were small. RBF-KPCA with default $\gamma$ was weakest for LR, $k$-NN, DT and RF. For the SVM cosine KPCA was best (mean macro-F1 0.657 versus 0.536 linear and 0.515 RBF; $\gamma$ untuned). Balanced class weights raised balanced accuracy for LR and SVM but macro-F1 only for SVM and DT; they lowered it for LR (0.736 $\to$ 0.710) and RF (0.901 $\to$ 0.885), so the best model remained the unweighted random forest."""))
R.append((seg("The serial benchmark (table in the repository)", "no real-time claim is made."),
r"""The serial benchmark (table in the repository) used 4{,}800 training and 1{,}200 test records. KPCA fitting took 5.5--5.6\,s against 0.02\,s for PCA, more than 200 times as long. It raised decision-tree training from 0.07\,s with PCA to 5.3--5.6\,s and random-forest training from 0.84--0.90\,s to 6.3--6.6\,s, and made random-forest inference about five times slower (0.092--0.123\,s against 0.020--0.021\,s per 1{,}200 records). Peak traced memory of the whole pipeline was 363\,MB for KPCA against 9.8\,MB for PCA or no reduction, about 37 times. It grew roughly quadratically with $n$ (3.1, 11.0, 41.6, 161.3 and 359.1\,MB for $n=400$ to $4800$; log--log slope 1.9), consistent with an $n\times n$ float64 kernel matrix (184\,MB at $n=4800$). As arithmetic, not a measurement, one dense float64 kernel matrix for all 2{,}830{,}743 source flows would need about 64\,TB, so exact KPCA on the full dataset would require approximation or sub-sampling (not tested). Timings come from a shared 2-vCPU machine; differences below about a factor of two should not be over-interpreted, and we make no real-time claim."""))
R.append(("\\textbf{Untested explanation.} A few leading components may discard discriminative low-variance directions.",
r"""\textbf{Possible explanations (untested).} We ran no experiment to separate the following hypotheses. (i) Trees split on single features, so rotating the data into principal components may bury the axis-aligned thresholds, for example on packet or flag counts, that trees can exploit; this would fit the clear losses for DT and RF. (ii) PCA keeps the directions of largest variance, and these need not be the directions that separate rare classes such as the web attacks. (iii) For the SVM, a default RBF kernel with $\gamma=$ ``scale'' on 78 standardised features may be poorly matched to the data. The gain from cosine KPCA fell from 0.076 to 0.021 once $C$ and $\gamma$ were tuned, which is consistent with this but does not prove it."""))
R.append((seg("On a 6{,}000-record sample drawn from all eight CIC-IDS2017 files", "to this sample and to CIC-IDS2017 only."),
r"""We asked whether PCA or KPCA improves intrusion detection over the 78 original CIC-IDS2017 features. On a class-stratified sample of 6{,}000 records, mostly it did not. A random forest on the original features scored highest (macro-F1 $0.901\pm0.020$; accuracy $0.972\pm0.004$; $0.900\pm0.022$ with nested selection). Reduction to 5--30 components was clearly worse for the decision tree and random forest, and at best practically equal for logistic regression (30 components) and $k$-NN (RQ1, RQ2). Only the RBF-SVM benefited, from cosine KPCA, and that advantage shrank from 0.076 to 0.021 after tuning. Linear KPCA coincided with PCA, and for LR and SVM the number of components mattered more than the kernel (RQ3). At 4{,}800 records KPCA needed more than 200 times the reducer fit time of PCA and about 37 times the traced memory (RQ4). The ranking held across repeated folds, nested selection, tuning, smaller training sets and the two row-order checks, which assume without proof that row order follows capture time (RQ5). Accuracy hid weak detection of web brute force and XSS (F1 0.53 and 0.59). These findings are limited to this sample of CIC-IDS2017."""))

R.append(("Timings and memory come from one 2-vCPU virtual machine, memory is a traced lower bound, and the 64\\,TB figure is arithmetic, not a measurement.",
r"Timings and memory come from one shared 2-vCPU virtual machine, memory is a traced lower bound, and differences below about a factor of two should not be over-interpreted; we make no real-time claim."))
R.append((" Timings come from a shared 2-vCPU machine; differences below about a factor of two should not be over-interpreted, and we make no real-time claim.",""))
R.append(("These checks assume that row order follows capture time and score only a subset of classes, so they support the ranking but give no precise penalty (tables in the repository).",
r"They assume that row order follows capture time and score only a subset of classes, so they support the ranking but give no precise penalty."))
R.append((seg("Fig.~\\ref{fig:cmp} and Table~\\ref{tab:lit} show reported accuracy side by side.","as our per-class results show."),
r"""Fig.~\ref{fig:cmp} and Table~\ref{tab:lit} put reported accuracies side by side. The recent studies quote 97.75--99.9965\,\% on their own datasets and splits; our random forest reaches 97.2\,\% accuracy but 0.901 macro-F1 on a sample that over-represents attacks. These figures are not comparable. They illustrate only that a high accuracy says little about minority-class detection: ours is 0.972 with web-attack F1 of 0.53 and 0.59."""))
R.append((seg("\\textbf{Why accuracy misleads.}","is about 0.02."),
r"""\textbf{Why accuracy misleads.} Benign flows are 67.2\,\% of the sample, so a trivial classifier already scores 0.672 accuracy, and the best model's 0.972 coexists with web-attack F1 of only 0.53 and 0.59. Accuracy also cannot show whether a one-point difference is noise; the fold standard deviation of macro-F1 is about 0.02."""))
R.append((seg("\\subsection{All executions in one chart}","tree-based classifiers lead overall."),
r"""Fig.~\ref{fig:all} shows all 25 classifier-by-space executions at 10 components (macro-F1, mean $\pm$ std over 15 folds; the black bar is the original space). Only for the SVM does a reduced space (cosine KPCA) exceed the original, and the tree-based classifiers lead overall."""))
R.append(("We propose no new algorithm and claim no state-of-the-art, real-time, production-ready or generalizable performance.","We propose no new algorithm. We claim no state-of-the-art, real-time or production-ready performance, and no generalization beyond this sample."))
for a, b in R:
    assert t.count(a) == 1, a[:60]
    t = t.replace(a, b)
open("main.tex", "w").write(t); print("applied", len(R))
